    <string name="check_cycling_title">Burner starts</string>
    <string name="check_cycling_value">%1$s starts per burner hour · %2$s starts and %3$s h a day</string>
    <string name="check_cycling_good">The burner runs in long, calm cycles. That is easy on the boiler and efficient.</string>
    <string name="check_cycling_bad">The burner switches on and off often (short cycling). That wears the burner and wastes gas.</string>
    <string name="check_cycling_suggest">Usually helps: lower the heating curve a little so the burner runs longer at low power, keep the night setback small, and keep most radiator valves open.</string>

    <string name="check_modulation_title">Burner power</string>
    <string name="check_modulation_value">Average %1$s %% while running · %2$s %% of the time near the minimum</string>
    <string name="check_modulation_good">The boiler adapts its power to the demand.</string>
    <string name="check_modulation_low">The boiler mostly runs at its lowest power: the house needs less heat than the boiler\'s minimum output, which causes frequent starts.</string>
    <string name="check_modulation_suggest">Lower supply temperatures (a lower heating curve) let it run longer and start less often.</string>

